---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Screenshot from '../components/Screenshot.astro';
import Command from '../components/Command.astro';
import Limits from '../components/Limits.astro';
import { RELEASE, REPO_URL } from '../site';
---

<Base
  fullTitle="A modern, private email client for Linux · OpenWren"
  description="OpenWren is a modern, open-source email client for Linux: apt and dnf repositories and an AppImage, mail encrypted on your computer, search in milliseconds."
>
  <section class="page-intro">
    <h1>A modern, private email client for Linux</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      OpenWren is a free, open-source email client that started on Linux. It looks like a program from this decade,
      installs from an apt or dnf repository, keeps your mail in an encrypted database on your machine, and searches
      200,000 messages in about 30 ms.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button" href="/download/#linux">Install on Linux</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href={REPO_URL}>Source code</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page-body">
    <Screenshot src="inbox.png" alt="The OpenWren inbox on Linux: folders, the message list and an open message" label="Inbox" width={760} />

    <h2>Why another Linux email client</h2>
    <p>
      I wanted a good, modern mail program on Linux and could not find one I was happy to use every day. The
      established ones do a great deal but show their age, and the newer ones are often not open source or want an
      account of their own. So I built the one I was missing, and I am sharing it.
    </p>

    <h2>Install</h2>
    <h3>Debian, Ubuntu and derivatives</h3>
    <Command code="curl -fsSL https://packages.openwren.app/key.gpg | sudo tee /usr/share/keyrings/openwren.gpg > /dev/null" />
    <Command code={'echo "deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/openwren.gpg] https://packages.openwren.app/apt stable main" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/openwren.list'} />
    <Command code="sudo apt update && sudo apt install openwren" />
    <h3>Fedora</h3>
    <Command code="sudo dnf config-manager addrepo --from-repofile=https://packages.openwren.app/rpm/openwren.repo" />
    <Command code="sudo dnf install openwren" />
    <h3>Any other distribution</h3>
    <p>
      Download the <a href={RELEASE.appImage}>AppImage</a>, make it executable and run it. The
      <a href="/download/#linux">download page</a> has the signing key's fingerprint and the steps to uninstall.
    </p>
    <p>
      The repositories are signed, and updates arrive with your normal system updates. Passwords and the database key
      are kept in the system keyring (GNOME Keyring, KWallet or another Secret Service provider).
    </p>

    <h2>What you get</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>One inbox and one search box</strong> for Gmail and any IMAP account. <a href="/search/">How search works</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Encrypted storage.</strong> Mail and its search index are in one SQLCipher database.</li>
      <li><strong>Trackers removed</strong>, remote images blocked until you ask, and warnings on mail that fails its sender checks.</li>
      <li><strong>Small and quick.</strong> A Rust engine and the system's WebKitGTK instead of a bundled browser: a 14 MB download that starts in about 0.6 s. <a href="/benchmarks/">Benchmarks</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Proton Mail</strong> works through <a href="/docs/#proton-mail-with-proton-mail-bridge">Proton Mail Bridge</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>OpenWren next to other Linux email clients</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>OpenWren</th><th>Thunderbird</th><th>Evolution</th><th>Geary</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Open source</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Mail encrypted on disk by the app</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Exchange / Microsoft 365</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Calendar and contacts</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
          <tr><td>PGP</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="muted">
      To the best of my knowledge in November 2026. If a cell is wrong or out of date, please
      <a href={`${REPO_URL}/issues`}>say so</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      In short: if you need a calendar, Exchange or PGP, use Thunderbird or Evolution. If you want mail only, with a
      modern interface, encrypted storage and a search you can rely on, try OpenWren. More in
      <a href="/thunderbird-alternative/">OpenWren as a Thunderbird alternative</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>What it does not do</h2>
    <Limits />
  </section>
</Base>
